WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.483
DNA strands run in opposite directions.

00:00:03.483 --> 00:00:05.577
This is called antiparallel.

00:00:05.577 --> 00:00:09.623
Notice that the template runs from three prime to five prime,

00:00:09.623 --> 00:00:13.968
while the complementary strand runs from five prime to three prime.

00:00:14.975 --> 00:00:18.564
New DNA grows from five prime to three prime.

00:00:18.564 --> 00:00:21.618
Each new nucleotide joins the three prime end of the

00:00:21.618 --> 00:00:24.018
growing complementary strand.

00:00:24.018 --> 00:00:29.362
The labels describe the strand ends, not two possible directions of synthesis.

00:00:30.392 --> 00:00:35.293
DNA polymerase cannot start a complementary strand from scratch.

00:00:35.293 --> 00:00:38.849
Primase makes a short RNA primer on the template.

00:00:38.849 --> 00:00:42.692
DNA polymerase can then extend the primer by adding DNA

00:00:42.692 --> 00:00:44.995
nucleotides at its three prime end.

00:00:46.017 --> 00:00:50.304
Remember: new DNA grows five prime to three prime.

00:00:50.304 --> 00:00:54.492
DNA polymerase adds to the three prime end of an existing primer.
